          %h4#ForComplexKTheory For complex K-theory
          %p
            Intermediate Jacobians of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/K-theory"} K-theory
            classes were considered in the
            = succeed "-style" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Witten99"} Witten 99, section 4.3
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#MooreWitten99"} Moore-Witten 99, section 3
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#MW00"} DMW 00, section 7.1
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#BelovMooreII"} Belov-Moore 06b, section 5
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantization"} quantization
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/RR-field"} RR-field
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type+II+superstring+theory"} type II superstring theory
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theory"} self-dual higher gauge theory
            (see there at
            = succeed ")." do
              %em
                %a{:href => "self-dual%20higher%20gauge%20theory#RRFieldsin10d"} Examples – RR-fields in 10d
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#MPS11"} MPS 11
          %h2#properties Properties
          %h3#RelationBetweenGriffithsAndWeilStructure Relation between the Griffiths and the Weil complex structure
          %p
            While the Griffiths complex structure on the intermediate Jacobian is not Kähler/not an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarized+variety"} algebraic polarization
            as the Weil complex structure is, it still has an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/p-convex+polarization"} p-convex polarization
            and there is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symplectomorphism"} symplectomorphism
            which is an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism"} isomorphism
            between the Griffiths and the Weil intermediate Jacobians as real symplectic manifolds
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi J
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi Griffiths
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi J
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi Weil
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (J^{k+1}(X), \omega_{Griffiths})
                  \\simeq
                  (J^{k+1}(X), \omega_{Weil})
                  \\,.
          %p
            This is due to (
            %a{:href => "#Griffiths68b"}> Griffiths 68b
            ), recalled as
            %a{:href => "#Griffiths12"} Griffiths 12 (2.6)
          %p (…)
          %h3#cycle_map__abeljacobi_map Cycle map / Abel-Jacobi map
          %p
            The intermediate Jacobians receive canonical maps from cycles (…) See at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Abel-Jacobi+map"} Abel-Jacobi map
          %h3#polarization Polarization
          %p
            For a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+manifold"} Hodge manifold
            the intermediate Jacobian canonically inherits the structure of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/polarized+variety"} polarized variety
          %h3#thetacharacteristics Theta-characteristics
          %p
            A certain
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/square+root"} square root
            of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/canonical+bundle"} canonical bundle
            on intermediate Jacobians – hence a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Theta+characteristic"} Theta characteristic
            – in dimension
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mi k
                  %mo +
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2k+1
            thought of as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/moduli+spaces"} moduli spaces
            of (flat)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+n-bundles+with+connection"} circle (2k+1)-bundles with connection
            yields the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partition+function"} partition function
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+theory"} self-dual higher gauge theory
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "#Witten96"} Witten 96
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#HopkinsSinger02"} Hopkins-Singer 02
          %h3#RelationToArtinMazurFormalGroups Relation to Artin-Mazur formal groups
          %p
            By theorem
            %a.maruku-ref{:href => "#DeligneTheorem"}
            the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/formal+geometry"} formal geometry
            of intermediate Jacobians around their canonical point is equivalently the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/deformation+theory"} deformation theory
            of
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Deligne+cohomology"} Deligne cohomology
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/line+n-bundles+with+connection"} line n-bundles with connection
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Artin-Mazur+formal+group"} Artin-Mazur formal group
            for
            %a{:href => "Artin-Mazur+formal+group#DeformationsOfDeligneCohomology"} deformations of Deligne cohomology
            (see there).
          %h2#examples Examples
          %p
            The two extreme cases of intermediate Jacobians
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
